# Slack MCP server configuration (amazing-slack-mcp).
# Copy to .env and fill in. NEVER commit .env, and never paste the token into a chat.
#
# The server speaks AS YOU: it only accepts the "User OAuth Token" (xoxp-…) of your own
# Slack app. Create ONE app per workspace (an app is never distributed):
#
#   1. https://api.slack.com/apps → Create New App → From scratch.
#      Name: "Alejandro AI" (any name — Slack does not show it on your messages; see
#      SLACK_SIGNATURE below). Pick the workspace. One app per workspace — a second workspace is a second app.
#   2. OAuth & Permissions → User Token Scopes (NOT Bot Token Scopes) → add these 15:
#        channels:read    channels:history  groups:read   groups:history
#        im:read          im:history        im:write      mpim:read
#        mpim:history     mpim:write        chat:write    reactions:write
#        users:read       users:read.email  search:read
#      (the same list the slack_health_check tool compares against). Add nothing else:
#      a scope can never be removed from an issued token, only by re-creating the app.
#      (The signature's bot user, under SLACK_SIGNATURE below, needs one BOT scope.)
#   3. Token rotation: leave it OFF. Once turned on it cannot be turned off, and a
#      rotating token (xoxe.xoxp-…) expires every 12 h — this server cannot refresh it.
#   4. Manage Distribution: leave public distribution OFF (it would make the app
#      "unlisted" and cut its rate limits).
#   5. Install to Workspace → copy the "User OAuth Token" (xoxp-…) into SLACK_USER_TOKEN
#      (never the "Bot User OAuth Token", xoxb-…, if the app has a bot user).
#      Added a scope later? Re-install the app and copy the token again.
#
# Refused at the first call, with a message naming the kind (the value is never echoed):
# bot tokens (xoxb-…), app-level tokens (xapp-…), browser session tokens (xoxc-/xoxd-…).

# User OAuth Token of YOUR app, sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Required.
SLACK_USER_TOKEN=

# Kill-switch. Leave unset for a read-only server. Set to 1 to allow posting, replying,
# editing, scheduling, reacting and opening DMs — and even then every write must pass the
# allowlists below.
# SLACK_ALLOW_WRITES=1

# Comma-separated conversation IDs (C…, G…, D…) a write may target, e.g.
# C0123ABCDEF,G0456GHIJKL. IDs only — a #name never matches. A channel or group DM shared
# with another organisation (Slack Connect) is writable ONLY by its explicit ID here.
# SLACK_WRITE_CHANNELS=

# Comma-separated user IDs (U…, W…). A DM or group DM is writable when every OTHER member
# is listed; slack_open_conversation may only open conversations with these people. Your
# own self-DM is always writable once SLACK_ALLOW_WRITES=1. An external person (another
# organisation) can only be written to via their own user id here (a 1:1 DM).
# SLACK_WRITE_USERS=

# Optional signature. Slack does not label these messages: they look exactly like ones you
# typed (measured with an internal app, in a DM and in a channel). To let teammates tell
# them apart, set SLACK_SIGNATURE. Every message the server posts, replies with,
# schedules or edits then goes out as Block Kit blocks: the text, then the signature in a
# context block under it, in the small grey font of Slack's own labels. Slack also gets a
# plain-text fallback (notifications, search, read-back) that ends with the signature
# after a blank line ("\n\n" + signature). An edited message keeps it exactly once; it is
# never added twice. It counts toward Slack's length limits, and a context block holds at
# most 3,000 characters. Write it in Slack mrkdwn (*bold*, <@U…> mentions, <url|label>
# links). Keep it on one line: a multi-line value is joined into one.
#
# Recommended: a bold label, like Slack's native one, then a mention of your app's bot
# user. Slack renders <@U…> as the blue "@Alejandro AI" tag, and clicking it opens the
# app's profile inside Slack (a link opens the web). The app gets a bot user only so it
# can be mentioned: its bot token is never used, and this server refuses it anyway.
#   a. https://api.slack.com/apps → your app → App Manifest → add, keeping the user scopes
#      as they are:
#        features:
#          bot_user:
#            display_name: Alejandro AI
#            always_online: false
#        oauth_config:
#          scopes:
#            bot:
#              - users:read
#      One bot scope, users:read, because Slack does not install a bot user without one.
#   b. Save, then Install App → Reinstall to Workspace. SLACK_USER_TOKEN stays the User
#      OAuth Token (xoxp-…; copy it again if it changed), never the new xoxb-… token.
#   c. Find the bot user's id (U…): slack_list_users with include_bots=true, or
#      slack_resolve_user with query "Alejandro AI" and include_bots=true.
# SLACK_SIGNATURE=*Sent using* <@U0BOTID>
#
# Fallback, for an app without a bot user: a link to your app's page, which renders
# "@Alejandro AI" in link colour like a tag but opens the web (the App ID is on the app's
# Basic Information page). Use your workspace's subdomain and App ID — for example, an
# app in the `acme` workspace is
#   *Sent using* <https://acme.slack.com/marketplace/A0123456789|@Alejandro AI>
# SLACK_SIGNATURE=*Sent using* <https://<workspace>.slack.com/marketplace/<APP_ID>|@Alejandro AI>
#
# Either way it only looks like Slack's label: it is message content, a marker and not
# provenance — anyone can type the same line, and any app can post the same block. In a
# shell, quote the value: * < > | are shell metacharacters.

# Web API base URL. Only override for proxies/testing.
# SLACK_API_URL=https://slack.com/api

# Per-request timeout in seconds.
# SLACK_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# Test-suite switch for the mutating `live_write` tests (they open YOUR OWN self-DM and
# post/update/react there — never a channel, never another person). It is NOT read from
# this file: export it inline on the command line, never in .env:
#   SLACK_TEST_ALLOW_WRITES=1 uv run pytest -m live_write
